/P 319 0 R
/E (The SafeGuard workflow is initiated by a multi-layered authentication process through Firebase, ensuring that only verified users can access the personalized safety dashboard. Upon reaching the dashboard, the application enters a "Standby-Ready" state, where it monitors for user input or sensor-based triggers. When the user activates the SOS button, a five-second cancellable countdown begins, providing a buffer against accidental triggers. If not cancelled, the system simultaneously executes four primary threads: siren activation, SMS dispatch, location sharing, and covert evidence recording.)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
2319 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 319 0 R
/E (The SMS service fetches the primary emergency contacts from the local SQLite database and appends a real-time Google Maps link to the distress message. Concurrently, the background camera module begins capturing images at intervals, storing them locally for forensic use. In parallel, the app initiates a direct VoIP or cellular call to the ERSS \(112\) or the user's designated primary contact.)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
2320 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 319 0 R
/E (The disarm workflow requires a specific numeric sequence \(245324\) to prevent an attacker from silencing the device manually. For preemptive safety, the user can also trigger the "Fake Call" workflow, which simulates a realistic incoming call to deter potential harassers. This integrated flow ensures that the transition from a state of safety to an emergency response is instantaneous and multi-faceted.)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
