4861 0 obj
<<
/Title (The development of SafeGuard followed a rigorous Software Development Life Cycle \(SDLC\) centered on the "Resilience-by-Design" principle. The initial phase involved a comprehensive threat modeling exercise to identify common failure points in emergency scenarios, such as loss of internet or physical obstruction of the device. We selected React Native 0.84.1 as the core framework to ensure high-performance cross-platform compatibility while maintaining a single codebase for easier security auditing. TypeScript was mandated as the primary programming language to enforce a "Fail-Fast" development environment, catching potential data-type errors during compilation rather than at runtime. For the data layer, a hybrid architecture was implemented: SQLite handles relational emergency data for offline reliability, while Firebase manages cloud-based authentication. The user interface was designed following Material Design 3 guidelines, prioritizing large touch targets and high-contrast elements for accessibility under duress. To manage the complex state of an ongoing emergency, the React Context API was utilized to create a global "System-Wide SOS State" that overrides all other UI interactions. We specifically chose the react-native-vision-camera library for its ability to handle background frame processing without requiring a visible viewfinder component.)
/Dest [64 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 4855 0 R
/Next 4862 0 R
>>
endobj
4862 0 obj
<<
/Title (The implementation phase focused heavily on "Native Module Bridging" to bypass standard Android and iOS limitations. For instance, the SMS module was engineered to interface directly with the Telephony Manager to ensure that alerts are dispatched even if the default messaging app is unresponsive. The location service employs a "High-Accuracy-First" strategy, polling both GPS satellites and Wi-Fi triangulation to provide coordinates with a margin of error of less than 10 meters. To protect the user's data, all local SQLite databases are encrypted, and sensitive contact information is never shared with third-party analytics. We implemented a "Fail-Safe Countdown" of five seconds to allow users to cancel accidental triggers, thereby maintaining the integrity of the system's alerts. The disarm mechanism was built using a persistent overlay that requires a specific six-digit PIN, ensuring that an attacker cannot simply "close" the app to stop the siren. Testing was conducted using the Jest framework for unit tests and manual stress testing on various hardware configurations, ranging from budget Android devices to flagship iPhones. This methodology ensures that every line of code serves the primary goal: providing a reliable, secure, and rapid response when a human life is at risk.)
/Dest [64 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 4855 0 R
/Prev 4861 0 R
>>
endobj
